\section{Introduction}

The Riemann Hypothesis (RH) states that all non‑trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta
function $\zeta(s)$ lie on the critical line $\Re(s)=1/2$. A celebrated equivalence
due to Weil \cite{Weil1952} and Bombieri \cite{Bombieri2000} relates RH to the
non‑negativity of the quadratic form
\[
\mathcal W(f) = \sum_{\rho} \wh f(\gamma_\rho)^2,
\]
where $f$ belongs to an admissible space $\Szero$ of test functions (even,
real‑valued Schwartz functions with $\wh f(\pm i/2)=0$), $\rho = \tfrac12 + i\gamma_\rho$
are the non‑trivial zeros, and $\gamma_\rho = (\rho - \tfrac12)/i$.

The Guinand–Weil explicit formula provides a decomposition
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Wdecomp}
\mathcal W(f) = \mathcal K(f) - \mathcal B(f),
\end{equation}
with an archimedean term $\mathcal K(f)$ and a prime‑sum term $\mathcal B(f)$.
Under RH, $\mathcal W(f) \ge 0$ for all admissible $f$, which yields the inequality
$\mathcal B(f) \le \mathcal K(f)$.

In this paper we construct a concrete family of admissible test functions
$\{f_{\gamma_0,\sigma}\}$ built from symmetrised Gaussians. For this family we
compute the autocorrelation $\Phi_{\gamma_0,\sigma} = f_{\gamma_0,\sigma} * \widetilde f_{\gamma_0,\sigma}$
in closed polynomial form. We then prove three new theorems:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The \emph{Shifted‑Gaussian Autocorrelation Decomposition} (Theorem~\ref{thm:decomp}),
          which gives the exact polynomial expression for $\Phi_{\gamma_0,\sigma}$,
          including both cosine and sine modulations.
    \item The \emph{Oscillatory‑Decoupling Bound with Explicit Constant} (Theorem~\ref{thm:decouple}),
          which quantifies the exponential suppression of cross terms.
    \item The \emph{Complex‑Frequency Amplification Identity} (Proposition~\ref{prop:complexamp}),
          which evaluates $\wh f_{\gamma_0,\sigma}$ at a complex spectral point
          $z_0 = \gamma_0 - i\delta$ and provides explicit error bounds.
\end{enumerate}

Using these results, we derive a fully explicit conditional inequality for weighted
prime sums under RH (Theorem~\ref{thm:RHineq}). The explicit nature of the formulas
makes them suitable for numerical verification and pedagogical exposition.

\section{Preliminaries}

\subsection{Fourier transform convention}
We use the convention
\[
\wh f(\xi) = \int_{\R} f(x) e^{-i\xi x}\,dx,\qquad
f(x) = \frac1{2\pi} \int_{\R} \wh f(\xi) e^{i\xi x}\,d\xi.
\]
